<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Nullroute — the DAC half of the /data/misc/nullroute access story.
    Installs to /system_ext/etc/permissions/nullroute-gid.xml

    THE PROBLEM
    -----------
    /data/misc is mode 01771 system:misc. Nothing that is not uid system, uid
    root, or a member of gid misc (AID_MISC = 9998) can even traverse it, let
    alone reach /data/misc/nullroute/{index,ctl,log,priv} at 0770 system:misc.

    SELinux is only half the gate. A process can hold
    `allow nullroute_app nullroute_ctl_file:file { ... map ... }` and still get
    EACCES from ordinary Unix permissions. Both halves have to line up.

    THE REJECTED FIX
    ----------------
    android:sharedUserId="android.uid.system" would give the app uid 1000 and
    solve this in one line. It is refused:
      * deprecated and permanently un-migratable once shipped;
      * it collapses the app into the system UID's sandbox, so a bug in the
        blocklist parser — which chews on hundreds of thousands of lines of
        attacker-influenced text from the internet — becomes a system-UID
        compromise;
      * it interferes with the /data/app sideload-update path we rely on for
        out-of-band APK updates.
    The sibling in-tree app packages/apps/Freezer correctly does not use it.

    THE FIX
    -------
    SystemConfig parses <permission> elements with <group gid="..."/> children
    from every etc/permissions directory it reads with the ALLOW_PERMISSIONS
    flag, and /system_ext/etc/permissions is read with that flag. PackageManager
    then adds the mapped gid to the supplementary group list of any package
    holding the permission. This is the same machinery that maps
    android.permission.INTERNET -> gid inet; we are not inventing a mechanism.

    The app must ALSO declare the permission itself, with
    android:protectionLevel="signature", and <uses-permission> it. The signature
    check is trivially satisfied — the app is its own signer — and declaring it
    in the manifest means the permission exists even if this file is somehow not
    read, which turns a silent gid failure into a loud missing-permission one.

    VERIFY ON DEVICE (Spike 2, SPEC.md §10.6):
        adb shell cat /proc/$(adb shell pidof com.bestrom.nullroute)/status | grep Groups
    must list 9998.

    FALLBACK IF THE MAPPING DOES NOT TAKE
    -------------------------------------
    Relax the four state dirs from 0770 to 0775 in rom/init.nullroute.rc and let
    SELinux be the sole gate. That is safe here specifically because the parent
    /data/misc is 01771 and therefore already unreachable by untrusted_app, and
    because MAC is enforcing on user builds. It is a fallback, not the design:
    defence in depth is the point of doing it properly.
-->
<permissions>
    <permission name="com.bestrom.nullroute.permission.STATE">
        <!-- AID_MISC = 9998, the group that owns /data/misc. -->
        <group gid="misc" />
    </permission>
</permissions>
